  "articleBody": "Thinking about making your stairs safer is a smart move, especially if you have an older home or anticipate changing mobility needs. Before diving into an upgrade, there are a few key things to consider. First, understand what constitutes a 'required' handrail in your area. While OSHA guidance typically requires handrails on stairways with four or more risers, residential building rules can vary across places like Denver, Castle Rock, or Colorado Springs. Do you need a rail on one side, or both? Next, think about what hazards an inspector might flag. Common issues include gaps in rail continuity, incorrect height, or insufficient wall clearance. You'll also want to consider the material and grip comfort of new handrails, as well as secure anchorage. If your upgrade involves structural framing changes, or if you're aiming for accessibility modifications, this can trigger permit reviews. This is where a professional eye becomes invaluable to navigate codes and ensure the upgrade truly enhances safety and isn't just a cosmetic fix.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}